  "query_preview": "/Through Sunday/...\nIssued at 1243 AM MDT Mon Aug 3 2026\n\nWe will kick off the work week with one more day of hotter than\nnormal temperatures before a cold front brings some relief for\nTuesday and Wednesday. The Heat Advisory remains in place through\n6 PM tonight along the I-25 corridor and Weld County. With poor\novernight recoveries (much of the lower elevations remain in the\n70s and 80s as of 230 AM), it won't take long for temperatures to\nheat back up to uncomfortable values. Of course, there is the\npossibility that the cold front picks up its pace and arrives\nsooner than expected, which would prevent temperatures from\nheating to the current forecast highs. Considering temperatures\nare a few degrees warmer tonight compared to this time yesterday,\nand most locations hit the mid 90s by early afternoon on Sunday,\nconfidence remains fairly high that most locations will reach the\ntriple digits before the front arrives, with highest confidence\nfor locations from Fort Collins and Greeley southward across \nmetro Denver. While most people will be happy to see a few days of\nrelief from the heat, we are expecting smoke concentrations to \nincrease with the approaching airmass from fires outside our \nregion. \n\nPrecipitation chances will remain fairly low today, with the\ngeneral expectation being that high-based showers will develop \nearly afternoon across the higher elevations and bring gusty \noutflow winds and microburst potential as they pass by. DCAPE\nvalues will range from 1500-2000 J/kg, which would make for \npotential for an isolated severe gust, but expecting 35-45 mph\ngusts with the majority of what develops. If any precipitation\ndoes manage to make it to the ground, it is expected to remain\nvery light (a few hundredths). Winds are expected to become gusty\nwith the frontal passage that will likely see some localized \nelevated to near-critical fire weather conditions until relative \nhumidity has time to improve behind the front. There is a slight \nchance that a few thunderstorms develop along the front that could\nlead to some dry lightning concerns as well since little \nprecipitation is expected. Overnight lows tonight are expected to \nbe about 10-15 degrees cooler than Sunday night across the lower \nelevations. \n\nOn Tuesday, northern Colorado will sit on the southern edge of a\n40 kt 500 mb jet that will increase winds across the higher\nelevations. While the lower elevations will see much cooler \ntemperatures compared to the past few days, as 700 mb temperatures\ncool by about 10C over the plains, there will be far less cooling\nacross the mountain valleys (700 mb temps only cool by a few\ndegrees C). Afternoon highs will struggle to get out of the 70s \nacross the lower elevations, where North and Middle park will warm\ninto the mid 80s. With the increasing winds expected across our \nmountain valleys coinciding with relative humidities in the mid-\nteens, we are expecting elevated to critical fire weather \nconditions to develop in North and Middle Park in the afternoon. \nThe jet is expected to drop slightly southward on Wednesday that \nwill keep critical fire weather concerns in place for the higher \nelevations once again, where relative humidities are expected to \ndrop into the single-digits. \n\nPrecipitation chances will remain limited through the week, with\nthe best chances for any accumulations expected over the Central\nMountains on Tuesday afternoon, and our northeastern corner on\nWednesday. By Thursday, the ridge is expected to rebuild over the\nwestern CONUS and temperatures are expected to gradually increase\neach day until they approach the triple-digits again by next\nweekend.",
